{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "*highlighted section part 1*  \nRaymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He was born in 1140 and was the son of Raymond II and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His early life was shaped by the turbulent politics of the Crusader states. Raymond became count at the age of twelve after his father was assassinated in 1152. Due to his young age a regency was established led by his mother and later by the King of Jerusalem. This period was marked by instability as rival factions vied for control of the county. Raymond was eventually declared of age in 1163 and began to rule independently.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*  \nRaymond III was known for his diplomatic skills and his cautious approach to warfare. He often preferred negotiation over conflict which set him apart from other Crusader nobles. He formed alliances with Muslim leaders including Nur ad-Din and later Saladin. His relationship with Saladin was particularly notable as it allowed for periods of peace along the borders of Tripoli. Raymond also played a key role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was appointed regent of Jerusalem after the death of Baldwin IV in 1183. As regent he faced opposition from powerful nobles and was eventually replaced by Guy of Lusignan. His removal from power was controversial and contributed to the growing divisions among the Crusader leadership.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*  \nRaymond III was deeply involved in the events leading up to the Battle of Hattin in 1187. He opposed Guy of Lusignan’s aggressive campaign against Saladin and warned against attacking the Muslim forces at Tiberias. His advice was ignored and he was forced to join the crusader army against his better judgment. At Hattin Raymond fought bravely but the crusaders suffered a crushing defeat. He survived the battle and fled to Tripoli. He died later that 